Well, that is a little bit complicated.  We load another library in our provider initialization that does a FIPS integrity check on its own and loads the default provider and fetches implicitly for an HMAC with sha256 in the default library context.  So I see two loads of the default provider, one load of our provider, but no queries for functions in our provider.  If I revert the mentioned commit, our provider testcases work again.  They use `EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_name` to get a context, initialize key generation and check that the context is associated with the proper provider:
```
    ctx = EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_name(NULL, algo, "?provider=ibmca");
    if (ctx == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_name failed\n");
        goto out;
    }

    if (EVP_PKEY_keygen_init(ctx) <= 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "EVP_PKEY_keygen_init failed\n");
        goto out;
    }

    provider = EVP_PKEY_CTX_get0_provider(ctx);
    if (provider == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Context is not a provider-context\n");
        goto out;
    }

    provname = OSSL_PROVIDER_get0_name(provider);
    if (strcmp(provname, "ibmca") != 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Context is not using the IBMCA provider, but '%s'\n",
               provname);
        goto out;
    }
```
Since the mentioned commit we get "default" as provider name.  There were no changes in the last couple of days to our provider, so I consider this a regression in OpenSSL.
